Design a modern, highly intuitive responsive web platform called “ReGround”.

ReGround is a planning-stage excavated-soil routing, verification and local reuse platform for New Zealand construction and earthworks projects.

The platform has TWO primary user groups:

1. SUPPLY SIDE
Civil contractors, earthworks contractors, developers, councils and infrastructure project teams that generate surplus excavated soil.

Their key goals are:
- know where surplus soil will go before excavation starts
- reduce disposal and transport costs
- avoid programme delays
- identify nearby receivers
- compare reuse versus disposal routes
- see whether the required testing and approvals are complete
- keep a fallback disposal route available

2. DEMAND SIDE
Construction companies, landscapers, earthworks projects, soil processors and local infrastructure projects that need soil, sand, clay, gravel or other excavated material.

Their key goals are:
- quickly find suitable materials nearby
- filter by material type, volume, location, date and specification
- understand material quality and verification status
- see testing and supporting documents
- compare transport distance and cost
- request or reserve suitable material
- know whether the material matches their acceptance criteria

IMPORTANT UX PRINCIPLE:
This is a professional B2B/B2G system, but it should feel as easy to use as Airbnb, Trade Me or a modern logistics marketplace.

Avoid complex engineering-software aesthetics.

Use:
- large readable typography
- simple cards
- clear status indicators
- map-based discovery
- progressive disclosure
- minimal technical language on the first layer
- more detailed technical information only when the user expands a material profile
- strong visual hierarchy
- clear primary call-to-action buttons
- mobile and desktop responsive layouts

BRAND:
Name: ReGround

Tagline:
“Know where your soil is going before you dig.”

Alternative commercial message:
“Reduce soil-management cost without adding programme risk.”

Visual style:
- clean New Zealand infrastructure / environmental technology aesthetic
- premium but practical
- soft white / warm grey backgrounds
- deep forest green
- muted earth brown / clay accents
- dark charcoal typography
- subtle blue-green map colours
- rounded cards
- generous whitespace
- simple line icons
- professional photography of construction, earthworks, soil and local landscapes
- avoid overly futuristic AI visuals
- avoid excessive gradients
- avoid dark dashboard-heavy interfaces

The product should visually communicate:
planning → verification → matching → transport → local reuse.

